Publick: what you may reuse, and how
====================================

What Publick makes is licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0
International license (CC BY 4.0). You may copy, share, and adapt it, for
any purpose, including commercially, as long as you give credit. The full
legal text is in LICENSE-CC-BY-4.0.txt, and a summary is at
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/.

What Publick makes
------------------

- The summaries of agendas and minutes, their headlines, and their lists of
  agenda items and decisions, in every language (towns/*/data/summaries/).
- The data Publick compiles from public sources: meeting listings and their
  history (towns/*/data/meetings/), the figures and tables built from
  government data, and the files each site publishes from them
  (/meetings/data/decisions.csv, /feed.xml, the CSV downloads).
- The wording of the sites.

Credit it like this, with a link to the document the summary came from:

    Summary by Publick (publick.org), AI-generated from [the city's agenda].

For data, "Data compiled by Publick (publick.org)" and a link to the page or
file you used is enough.

What isn't Publick's
--------------------

These keep their own terms. Publick's license covers only what Publick
added, such as a summary, or the way records are gathered and arranged.

- 311 requests (towns/*/data/311/ and the sites' 311 pages and downloads)
  come from SeeClickFix's public API and keep its license: CC BY-NC-SA 3.0
  (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/3.0/), which doesn't allow
  commercial use and asks that adaptations be shared under the same terms.
- Agendas, minutes, and other documents a city, town, or school district
  publishes are public records. That includes their full text on the sites,
  whether laid out from the document's PDF or transcribed by AI from a scan.
- U.S. Census Bureau and Bureau of Labor Statistics figures are in the
  public domain.
- State figures (such as the Massachusetts Division of Local Services
  reports in states/ma/) keep the terms of the state that publishes them.
- Ward maps keep the terms of the city that publishes them, and street maps
  are OpenStreetMap's (https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright).

Code
----

The code in this repository (scripts/, home/, the Worker settings, and the
workflows) is under the MIT License, the same as the Publick engine's:
https://github.com/publick-org/publick-engine/blob/main/LICENSE

A note on AI-written text
-------------------------

The summaries are written by AI and can be wrong. The city's document is the
official record, and every summary links to it. In the United States, text
written entirely by AI may have little copyright protection, so the credit
asked for above rests as much on custom as on law. Please give it anyway.
